messages.content:
I'll explore the ApiMonitoring implementation and how local network/socket events are logged elsewhere in the project.I have the full picture now. Here's what I found and what I'll change.

**Context (from the code)**
- `ApiMonitoringView.vue` + `useApiMonitoring.js` render **only** server-side aggregates from `/api/api-logs/*` (`src/api/modules/apiLogs.js`).
- Nothing in the app records the two streams that never reach a route handler: **socket.io traffic** (`SocketManager`) and **calls this tab makes to the local/LAN host** (`CodxJuniorConnection`).
- `useApiMonitoring.js` already owns all derived state/aggregations for the view (presentational components), so the new streams belong there too.

**Plan**
1. `src/api/telemetry.js` *(new)* — bounded in-memory ring buffers + counters for socket events and local-network calls; locality detection (`localhost`, RFC1918, `*.local`, same origin); stores only shape (name, direction, size, timings), never payloads.
2. `src/api/socket.js` — record lifecycle (`connect`, `disconnect`, `connect_error`, `reconnect`), inbound events (`onAny`) and outbound `emit` (ack latency when an ack callback is used).
3. `src/api/connection.js` — record each HTTP call (method, path, status, browser-measured latency) without altering the request chain.
4. `src/views/api-monitoring/useApiMonitoring.js` — expose the two logs, their totals and grouped rows, plus 2 diagnostics rules and `fmtClock`.
5. `src/views/api-monitoring/ApiMonitoringView.vue` — two new cards (“Socket events”, “Local network”) + header counters, following the existing card/table/KPI visuals.

<file_change path="src/api/telemetry.js" language="javascript" change_type="new" encoding="text">
/**
 * ============================================================================
 * Client telemetry — socket events and local-network calls.
 * ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * API monitoring reports what the SERVER measured for the requests it handled
 * (`/api/api-logs/*`). Two kinds of activity are invisible to that data source
 * because they never reach a route handler:
 *
 *   • SOCKET EVENTS — the socket.io traffic of this browser tab: what it emits,
 *     what it receives and the connection lifecycle. The server publishes the
 *     events but keeps no per-client delivery log.
 *   • LOCAL NETWORK CALLS — the HTTP requests this tab sends to the codx-junior
 *     host over the local network (same origin / localhost / RFC1918 / *.local),
 *     with the wall-clock latency the browser sees: DNS + TCP + round trip.
 *
 * Both streams are recorded IN THE BROWSER, in memory, as bounded ring buffers:
 * a session's worth of history at a fixed memory cost and with zero write
 * traffic. They are cleared from the API monitoring view on demand.
 *
 * PRIVACY / SIZE
 * --------------
 * Only the SHAPE of an event is stored: name, direction, payload size, timings
 * and error message. Payloads and request bodies are never copied here.
 * ============================================================================
 */
import { reactive } from 'vue'

/** Ring-buffer size, per stream. Enough to explain a session, bounded memory. */
export const CLIENT_LOG_LIMIT = 300

/** Calls slower than this are counted as "slow" in the header of the card. */
export const SLOW_CALL_MS = 1000

/* ── Local network detection ──────────────────────────────────────────────── */

/**
 * Hostnames/IPs that mean "the machine next to me". Single-label hosts
 * ("codx", "nas") and `.local` / `.lan` / `.internal` names are LAN names; the
 * address ranges are the RFC1918 / link-local blocks.
 */
const PRIVATE_HOST_PATTERNS = [
  /^localhost$/i,
  /^127\./,
  /^10\./,
  /^192\.168\./,
  /^172\.(1[6-9]|2\d|3[01])\./,
  /^169\.254\./, // link-local (APIPA)
  /^\[?::1\]?$/,
  /^\[?f[cd][0-9a-f]{2}:/i, // IPv6 unique-local / link-local
  /\.(local|localdomain|lan|internal|home|corp)$/i
]

function browserLocation() {
  return typeof window !== 'undefined' ? window.location : null
}

function currentOrigin() {
  const location = browserLocation()
  return location?.origin || 'http://localhost'
}

function currentHref() {
  const location = browserLocation()
  return location?.href || 'http://localhost/'
}

/**
 * True when the hostname belongs to the local network.
 * @param {string} hostname
 * @returns {boolean}
 */
export function isLocalHostname(hostname) {
  const host = String(hostname || '').toLowerCase()
  if (!host) return false
  // The host serving this tab is, by definition, the local codx-junior host.
  if (browserLocation() && host === browserLocation().hostname.toLowerCase()) return true
  // Single-label host ("codx", "nas", "workstation") — a LAN name, not a FQDN.
  if (!host.includes('.') && !host.includes(':')) return true
  return PRIVATE_HOST_PATTERNS.some((pattern) => pattern.test(host))
}

/**
 * True when `url` targets the local network (relative URLs resolve against the
 * current document, so every same-origin API call qualifies).
 * @param {string} url
 * @returns {boolean}
 */
export function isLocalNetworkUrl(url) {
  let parsed
  try {
    parsed = new URL(String(url || ''), currentHref())
  } catch {
    return false
  }
  if (parsed.origin === currentOrigin()) return true
  return isLocalHostname(parsed.hostname)
}

/**
 * Path of `url` without the query string.
 *
 * The query carries `codx_path` (project scoping) and search terms, so it is
 * useless for grouping and would explode the row count.
 * @param {string} url
 * @returns {string}
 */
export function localNetworkPath(url) {
  try {
    return new URL(String(url || ''), currentHref()).pathname || '/'
  } catch {
    return String(url || '').split('?')[0] || '/'
  }
}

/* ── Helpers ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */

/** Serializable size of a payload, in characters. Never throws. */
export function payloadSize(value) {
  if (value === undefined || value === null) return 0
  if (typeof value === 'string') return value.length
  try {
    return JSON.stringify(value)?.length || 0
  } catch {
    // Circular or non-serializable (class instance): size is unknown.
    return 0
  }
}

function nowMs() {
  return typeof performance !== 'undefined' && typeof performance.now === 'function'
    ? performance.now()
    : Date.now()
}

let sequence = 0

/** Newest entry first; the tail is dropped once the buffer is full. */
function pushCapped(list, entry) {
  list.unshift(entry)
  if (list.length > CLIENT_LOG_LIMIT) list.splice(CLIENT_LOG_LIMIT)
}

/* ── State ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */

/**
 * Reactive store shared by every producer (socket / http layer) and by the API
 * monitoring view. Counters are cumulative since page load and survive a buffer
 * rotation, so the totals never "go down" while the session lasts.
 */
export const telemetry = reactive({
  /** Socket events, newest first: { id, ts, event, direction, size, durationMs, error, detail, socketId }. */
  socket: [],
  /** Local network calls, newest first: { id, ts, method, path, status, durationMs, error }. */
  network: [],

  /** Socket connection state, mirrored from the SocketManager lifecycle. */
  socketConnected: false,
  socketId: null,

  socketCounters: {
    total: 0,
    sent: 0,
    received: 0,
    system: 0,
    errors: 0,
    ackTotalMs: 0,
    acked: 0
  },

  networkCounters: {
    calls: 0,
    errors: 0,
    totalMs: 0,
    slow: 0
  },

  lastSocketAt: null,
  lastNetworkAt: null
})

/* ── Producers ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */

/**
 * Records one socket event.
 *
 * @param {object} entry
 * @param {string} entry.event        Event name (or lifecycle name: connect…).
 * @param {'in'|'out'|'system'} [entry.direction='in']
 * @param {number} [entry.size]       Payload size in characters.
 * @param {number|null} [entry.durationMs] Ack latency, when an ack was used.
 * @param {string|null} [entry.error] Failure message, if any.
 * @param {string|null} [entry.detail] Extra context (e.g. disconnect reason).
 * @param {string|null} [entry.socketId]
 * @returns {object} the stored entry
 */
export function recordSocketEvent({
  event,
  direction = 'in',
  size = 0,
  durationMs = null,
  error = null,
  detail = null,
  socketId = null
} = {}) {
  const entry = {
    id: ++sequence,
    ts: Date.now(),
    event: String(event || 'unknown'),
    direction: direction === 'out' || direction === 'system' ? direction : 'in',
    size: Number(size) || 0,
    durationMs: durationMs === null || durationMs === undefined
      ? null
      : Math.max(0, Math.round(Number(durationMs) || 0)),
    error: error ? String(error) : null,
    detail: detail ? String(detail) : null,
    socketId: socketId || telemetry.socketId || null
  }

  pushCapped(telemetry.socket, entry)

  const counters = telemetry.socketCounters
  counters.total += 1
  if (entry.direction === 'out') counters.sent += 1
  else if (entry.direction === 'in') counters.received += 1
  else counters.system += 1
  if (entry.error) counters.errors += 1
  if (entry.durationMs !== null) {
    counters.ackTotalMs += entry.durationMs
    counters.acked += 1
  }

  telemetry.lastSocketAt = entry.ts
  return entry
}

/**
 * Records one HTTP call made by this tab, when it targets the local network.
 * Non-local URLs are ignored (they are not "local network" activity).
 *
 * @param {object} entry
 * @param {string} entry.method
 * @param {string} entry.url
 * @param {number} [entry.status]       HTTP status (0 when it never answered).
 * @param {number} [entry.durationMs]   Browser-measured wall-clock latency.
 * @param {Error|string|null} [entry.error]
 * @returns {object|null} the stored entry, or null when the URL is not local
 */
export function recordNetworkCall({
  method = 'GET',
  url = '',
  status = 0,
  durationMs = 0,
  error = null
} = {}) {
  if (!isLocalNetworkUrl(url)) return null

  const entry = {
    id: ++sequence,
    ts: Date.now(),
    method: String(method || 'GET').toUpperCase(),
    path: localNetworkPath(url),
    status: Number(status) || 0,
    durationMs: Math.max(0, Math.round(Number(durationMs) || 0)),
    error: error ? String(error.message || error) : null
  }

  pushCapped(telemetry.network, entry)

  const counters = telemetry.networkCounters
  counters.calls += 1
  counters.totalMs += entry.durationMs
  if (entry.error || entry.status >= 500) counters.errors += 1
  if (entry.durationMs >= SLOW_CALL_MS) counters.slow += 1

  telemetry.lastNetworkAt = entry.ts
  return entry
}

/** Mirrors the socket connection state (used by the "Connected" pill). */
export function setSocketConnected(connected, socketId = null) {
  telemetry.socketConnected = !!connected
  telemetry.socketId = connected ? (socketId || telemetry.socketId) : null
}

/* ── Maintenance ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */

/**
 * Empties one stream (or both) and resets its counters. The connection state is
 * intentionally kept: clearing the log must not lie about the socket.
 * @param {'socket'|'network'|'all'} [scope='all']
 */
export function clearClientTelemetry(scope = 'all') {
  const all = scope === 'all'

  if (all || scope === 'socket') {
    telemetry.socket.splice(0)
    Object.assign(telemetry.socketCounters, {
      total: 0, sent: 0, received: 0, system: 0, errors: 0, ackTotalMs: 0, acked: 0
    })
    telemetry.lastSocketAt = null
  }

  if (all || scope === 'network') {
    telemetry.network.splice(0)
    Object.assign(telemetry.networkCounters, { calls: 0, errors: 0, totalMs: 0, slow: 0 })
    telemetry.lastNetworkAt = null
  }
}

export default telemetry
</file_change>

<file_change path="src/api/socket.js" language="javascript" change_type="update" encoding="text">
import io from 'socket.io-client'
import {
  recordSocketEvent,
  payloadSize,
  setSocketConnected
} from './telemetry'

/**
 * SocketManager handles all socket.io connection lifecycle,
 * event emission and reception on behalf of the API layer.
 *
 * Every lifecycle change and every event that crosses the wire is also written
 * to the client telemetry ring buffer (`./telemetry`) — that is what
 * API monitoring → "Socket events" renders. Only the event name, the payload
 * size and the timings are kept; payloads are never copied.
 */
export class SocketManager {
  constructor({ onEvent, onConnect, onDisconnect } = {}) {
    this._socket = null
    this._onEvent = onEvent || (() => {})
    this._onConnect = onConnect || (() => {})
    this._onDisconnect = onDisconnect || (() => {})
    this._listeners = new Map()
  }

  get id() {
    return this._socket?.id || null
  }

  get connected() {
    return this._socket?.connected || false
  }

  get socket() {
    return this._socket
  }

  /**
   * Connect to the socket.io server.
   * If a socket already exists it will be closed first.
   */
  connect() {
    if (this._socket) {
      this._socket.close()
      this._socket = null
    }

    const socket = io({
      path: '/api/socket.io',
      reconnectionDelayMax: 5000,
      transports: ['websocket']
    })

    socket.on('connect_error', (err) => {
      console.warn('[SocketManager] connect_error:', err.message)
      recordSocketEvent({
        event: 'connect_error',
        direction: 'system',
        error: err?.message || 'connect_error',
        socketId: socket.id
      })
    })

    socket.on('connect', () => {
      console.log('[SocketManager] connected', socket.id)
      setSocketConnected(true, socket.id)
      recordSocketEvent({
        event: 'connect',
        direction: 'system',
        detail: socket.io?.engine?.transport?.name || null,
        socketId: socket.id
      })
      this._onConnect(socket.id)
    })

    socket.on('disconnect', (reason) => {
      console.log('[SocketManager] disconnected')
      setSocketConnected(false)
      recordSocketEvent({
        event: 'disconnect',
        direction: 'system',
        detail: reason || null,
        socketId: socket.id
      })
      this._onDisconnect()
    })

    socket.io.on('reconnect', (attempt) => {
      console.log('[SocketManager] reconnected', socket.id)
      setSocketConnected(true, socket.id)
      recordSocketEvent({
        event: 'reconnect',
        direction: 'system',
        detail: attempt ? `attempt ${attempt}` : null,
        socketId: socket.id
      })
      this._onConnect(socket.id)
    })

    socket.onAny((event, ...args) => {
      // Size of the whole argument list: the server may send more than one.
      recordSocketEvent({
        event,
        direction: 'in',
        size: payloadSize(args),
        socketId: socket.id
      })
      this._onEvent({ event, data: args[0] })
    })

    // Re-attach any named listeners registered before connect
    this._listeners.forEach((handler, event) => {
      socket.on(event, handler)
    })

    this._socket = socket
    return this
  }

  /**
   * Disconnect and destroy the current socket.
   */
  disconnect() {
    if (this._socket) {
      recordSocketEvent({
        event: 'close',
        direction: 'system',
        socketId: this._socket.id
      })
      setSocketConnected(false)
      this._socket.close()
      this._socket = null
    }
  }

  /**
   * Emit an event with optional data and an optional ack callback.
   * @param {string} event
   * @param {*} data
   * @param {Function} [ack]
   */
  emit(event, data, ack) {
    const socket = this._socket

    if (!socket) {
      console.warn('[SocketManager] emit called but socket is not connected')
      recordSocketEvent({
        event,
        direction: 'out',
        size: payloadSize(data),
        error: 'socket not connected'
      })
      return
    }

    if (typeof ack === 'function') {
      // The ack round trip is the only per-event latency this side can measure:
      // record it so a slow handler shows up in the socket table.
      const startedAt = typeof performance !== 'undefined' ? performance.now() : Date.now()
      socket.emit(event, data, (...ackArgs) => {
        const elapsed = (typeof performance !== 'undefined' ? performance.now() : Date.now()) - startedAt
        recordSocketEvent({
          event,
          direction: 'out',
          size: payloadSize(data),
          durationMs: elapsed,
          error: ackArgs[0]?.error || null,
          socketId: socket.id
        })
        ack(...ackArgs)
      })
    } else {
      recordSocketEvent({
        event,
        direction: 'out',
        size: payloadSize(data),
        socketId: socket.id
      })
      socket.emit(event, data)
    }
  }

  /**
   * Register a named event listener.
   * Persists across reconnects.
   * @param {string} event
   * @param {Function} handler
   */
  on(event, handler) {
    this._listeners.set(event, handler)
    if (this._socket) {
      this._socket.on(event, handler)
    }
  }

  /**
   * Remove a named event listener.
   * @param {string} event
   */
  off(event) {
    this._listeners.delete(event)
    if (this._socket) {
      this._socket.off(event)
    }
  }
}
</file_change>

<file_change path="src/api/connection.js" language="javascript" change_type="update" encoding="text">
import axios from 'axios'
import { recordNetworkCall } from './telemetry'


const interceptors = {
  request: [],
  response: []
}

export class CodxJuniorConnection {
  constructor({ settings, user } = {}) {
    this.user = user
    this.settings = settings || {}
    // Socket.IO session id associated with the current socket. It is kept in
    // sync by the API layer (see api.js `initSocket` / `initConnection`) and
    // sent as `x-sid` so the meets API can target signalling at *this* session
    // instead of every device logged in with the same account.
    this.sid = this.settings.sid || ''
    this.interceptors = interceptors
  }

  get headers() {
    return {
      "x-sid": this.sid || this.settings?.sid || '',
      "x-username": this.user?.username || '',
      "Authentication": `Bearer ${this.user?.token}`
    };
  }

  createConnection() {
    this.axiosInstance = axios.create({});
    this.liveRequests = 0;

    this.axiosInstance.interceptors.request.use(...interceptors.request);
    this.axiosInstance.interceptors.response.use(...interceptors.response);
  }

  prepareUrl(url) {
    const query = `codx_path=${encodeURIComponent(this.settings?.codx_path)}`;
    if (url.indexOf("codx_path") === -1) {
      let [path, params] = url.split("?");
      if (params) {
        params += `&${query}`;
      } else {
        params = query;
      }
      url = `${path}?${params}`;
      return url;
    }
    return url;
  }

  /**
   * Records the call in the client-side "Local network" log of API monitoring
   * and returns the SAME promise, so the request chain is untouched.
   *
   * What is kept: method, path, status and the wall-clock latency the browser
   * measured (DNS + TCP + round trip) — the part the server-side aggregates
   * cannot see. Bodies and responses never leave the request.
   *
   * @param {string} method
   * @param {string} url
   * @param {Promise} request
   * @returns {Promise} the same promise
   */
  trackLocalCall(method, url, request) {
    const hasPerf = typeof performance !== 'undefined' && typeof performance.now === 'function'
    const startedAt = hasPerf ? performance.now() : Date.now()
    const elapsed = () => (hasPerf ? performance.now() : Date.now()) - startedAt

    request.then(
      (response) => {
        recordNetworkCall({
          method,
          url,
          status: response?.status,
          durationMs: elapsed()
        })
      },
      (error) => {
        recordNetworkCall({
          method,
          url,
          status: error?.response?.status,
          durationMs: elapsed(),
          error
        })
      }
    )

    return request
  }

  get(url) {
    this.createConnection()
    this.liveRequests++;
    const request = this.axiosInstance.get(this.prepareUrl(url), { headers: this.headers })
    return this.trackLocalCall('GET', url, request)
      .then(({ data }) => data)
      .finally(() => this.liveRequests--);
  }

  del(url) {
    this.createConnection()
    this.liveRequests++;
    const request = this.axiosInstance.delete(this.prepareUrl(url), { headers: this.headers })
    return this.trackLocalCall('DELETE', url, request)
      .finally(() => this.liveRequests--);
  }

  post(url, data) {
    this.createConnection()
    this.liveRequests++;
    const request = this.axiosInstance.post(this.prepareUrl(url), data, { headers: this.headers })
    return this.trackLocalCall('POST', url, request)
      .then(({ data }) => data)
      .finally(() => this.liveRequests--);
  }

  put(url, data) {
    this.createConnection()
    this.liveRequests++;
    const request = this.axiosInstance.put(this.prepareUrl(url), data, { headers: this.headers })
    return this.trackLocalCall('PUT', url, request)
          .then(({ data }) => data)
          .finally(() => this.liveRequests--);
  }

  delete(url) {
    this.createConnection()
    this.liveRequests++;
    const request = this.axiosInstance.delete(this.prepareUrl(url), { headers: this.headers })
    return this.trackLocalCall('DELETE', url, request)
          .finally(() => this.liveRequests--);
  }
}
</file_change>

<file_change path="src/views/api-monitoring/useApiMonitoring.js" language="javascript" change_type="update" encoding="text">
/**
 * ============================================================================
 * API monitoring — view state, filters and derived aggregations.
 * ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * Every HTTP call handled by the backend is stored as one event and aggregated
 * server-side (see `/api/api-logs/*`). Unlike Infrastructure → Monitoring there
 * is NO client-side sampling buffer for those numbers: they come straight from
 * the stored history, so what the charts show is exactly what happened — nothing
 * is interpolated or invented.
 *
 * TWO EXTRA SOURCES ARE CLIENT-SIDE
 * ---------------------------------
 * Activity that never reaches a route handler is captured in the browser by
 * `@/api/telemetry` and rendered by the same view:
 *   • SOCKET EVENTS — what this tab emits/receives plus the connection
 *     lifecycle (the server keeps no per-client delivery log).
 *   • LOCAL NETWORK — the calls this tab makes to the codx-junior host on the
 *     LAN, with the latency the browser measured (DNS + TCP + round trip).
 * The module owns the whole view state (filters, last response, polling) so the
 * components stay presentational, the same split used by useInfra.js.
 *
 * LOADING MODEL
 * -------------
 * The six aggregations behind the view are INDEPENDENT queries. Each one is
 * committed the moment it answers, so the chart never waits behind a slow
 * table, and a query that hangs or fails only degrades its own card instead of
 * freezing the dashboard on an old snapshot.
 *
 * ADMIN MODE
 * ----------
 * `allUsers` switches every request to the `/admin` endpoints (metrics across
 * every user and project). It is only meaningful for admins — the view gates
 * the toggle on `$storex.users.isAdmin`.
 * ============================================================================
 */
import { computed, reactive } from 'vue'
import { API } from '@/api/api'
import { apiLogsModule } from '@/api/modules/apiLogs'
import { telemetry, clearClientTelemetry } from '@/api/telemetry'

/**
 * Time buckets offered by the header selector (`grouping` query param).
 * Second-level buckets are the useful ones for a live dashboard; `day` is kept
 * for long ranges. The value is forwarded verbatim to the backend.
 */
export const GROUPINGS = [
  { id: '5s', label: 'Every 5 sec' },
  { id: '10s', label: 'Every 10 sec' },
  { id: '30s', label: 'Every 30 sec' },
  { id: 'minute', label: 'Per minute' },
  { id: 'hour', label: 'Hourly' },
  { id: 'day', label: 'Daily' }
]

/** Bucket used until the operator picks another one. */
export const DEFAULT_GROUPING = '10s'

/** Sampling cadence of the auto-refresh loop (seconds). */
export const REFRESH_INTERVALS = [
  { value: 10, label: '10s' },
  { value: 30, label: '30s' },
  { value: 60, label: '1 min' },
  { value: 300, label: '5 min' }
]

/** Range shortcuts. "24h" resolves to yesterday→today (buckets are per day). */
export const DATE_PRESETS = [
  { id: 'today', label: 'Today' },
  { id: '24h', label: '24h' },
  { id: '7d', label: '7d' },
  { id: '30d', label: '30d' }
]

/** HTTP methods offered by the filter ('' = every method). */
export const METHODS = ['GET', 'POST', 'PUT', 'PATCH', 'DELETE']

export const SLOWEST_LIMIT = 25
export const ERRORS_LIMIT = 50
export const TOP_ENDPOINTS = 12
export const TOP_SLOWEST = 8
/** Rows rendered per client-side table (socket events / local network). */
export const TOP_CLIENT_ROWS = 8
export const DEFAULT_INTERVAL = 30

/**
 * Series rendered by the chart: throughput on the left axis (counts), latency
 * on the right one (ms) — the same palette as the infra / analytics charts.
 */
export const CHART_METRICS = [
  { key: 'calls', label: 'Calls', color: '#6366f1', fill: true },
  { key: 'errors', label: 'Errors', color: '#ef4444' },
  { key: 'avg_ms', label: 'Avg latency', color: '#10b981', axis: 'y1' },
  { key: 'p95_ms', label: 'p95 latency', color: '#f59e0b', axis: 'y1' }
]

/* ── Dates ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
export function isoDay(date) {
  return date.toISOString().split('T')[0]
}

export function todayISO() {
  return isoDay(new Date())
}

export function daysAgoISO(days) {
  const date = new Date()
  date.setDate(date.getDate() - days)
  return isoDay(date)
}

/* ── Client ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
let client = null

/**
 * Lazily instantiated API-log client. It shares the app-wide `API` instance, so
 * auth, base URL and interceptors all apply.
 */
function api() {
  if (!client) client = apiLogsModule(API)
  return client
}

/** The endpoint group matching the current admin mode. */
function scopedApi() {
  return state.allUsers ? api().admin : api()
}

/* ── State ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
export const state = reactive({
  // idle | loading | ready | error
  status: 'idle',
  error: null,
  lastUpdated: null,

  /**
   * Monotonic counter, incremented once per committed refresh AND once per
   * configuration change.
   *
   * Derived lists are invalidated by the payload replacement itself, but a
   * canvas cannot be: `ApiMonitoringChart` binds to this counter so every round
   * produces exactly one redraw — even when two consecutive payloads happen to
   * carry the same values (identical arrays ⇒ no prop change ⇒ no redraw).
   */
  revision: 0,

  // admin endpoints (metrics from every user) — set from users.isAdmin
  allUsers: false,

  // auto-refresh
  live: false,
  interval: DEFAULT_INTERVAL,

  filters: {
    startDate: daysAgoISO(1),
    endDate: todayISO(),
    grouping: DEFAULT_GROUPING,
    path: '',
    method: '',
    statusCode: '',
    minDurationSeconds: '',
    username: '',
    projectName: ''
  },

  // last response
  summary: null,
  series: [],
  endpoints: {},
  statuses: {},
  slowest: [],
  errors: [],
  dates: [],

  /**
   * Selectable endpoints for the "path" filter. Kept across reloads: once a
   * path filter is applied the by-endpoint response only contains that one
   * endpoint, so the list would otherwise collapse to a single option.
   */
  endpointOptions: [],

  // endpoint table ordering: calls | errors | avg | p95 | max
  sortBy: 'p95'
})

/* ── Filters ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
function buildFilters() {
  const filters = state.filters
  const params = {
    startDate: filters.startDate || undefined,
    endDate: filters.endDate || undefined,
    path: filters.path || undefined,
    method: filters.method || undefined,
    statusCode: filters.statusCode === '' ? undefined : Number(filters.statusCode),
    minDurationSeconds:
      filters.minDurationSeconds === '' ? undefined : Number(filters.minDurationSeconds)
  }
  // The user-scoped endpoints already filter by the authenticated user; passing
  // `username` there is meaningless (and rejected as an unknown query param).
  if (state.allUsers) {
    params.username = filters.username || undefined
    params.projectName = filters.projectName || undefined
  }
  return params
}

/**
 * Patches the filters WITHOUT fetching (used on every keystroke / select).
 * Call `applyFilters()` to reload.
 *
 * The revision bump is what makes the configuration change visible
 * immediately: the endpoint table re-sorts and the canvas repaints as soon as
 * the control is used, without waiting for the next network round.
 */
export function setFilter(patch = {}) {
  Object.assign(state.filters, patch)
  state.revision += 1
}

/** True when at least one filter narrows the result set. */
export const filtersActive = computed(() => {
  const f = state.filters
  return !!(f.path || f.method || f.statusCode !== '' || f.minDurationSeconds !== '')
})

/** Range shortcut matching the current dates (or null). */
export const activePreset = computed(() => {
  const today = todayISO()
  const { startDate, endDate } = state.filters
  if (startDate === today && endDate === today) return 'today'
  if (startDate === daysAgoISO(1) && endDate === today) return '24h'
  if (startDate === daysAgoISO(7) && endDate === today) return '7d'
  if (startDate === daysAgoISO(30) && endDate === today) return '30d'
  return null
})

export const groupingLabel = computed(
  () => GROUPINGS.find(g => g.id === state.filters.grouping)?.label || 'Every 10 sec'
)

/** Applies a range shortcut. */
export function applyPreset(id) {
  const today = todayISO()
  const ranges = {
    today: { startDate: today, endDate: today },
    '24h': { startDate: daysAgoISO(1), endDate: today },
    '7d': { startDate: daysAgoISO(7), endDate: today },
    '30d': { startDate: daysAgoISO(30), endDate: today }
  }
  const range = ranges[id]
  if (range) Object.assign(state.filters, range)
  return applyFilters()
}

/** Resets every filter back to the default (last 24h, 10 s buckets). */
export function clearFilters() {
  Object.assign(state.filters, {
    startDate: daysAgoISO(1),
    endDate: todayISO(),
    grouping: DEFAULT_GROUPING,
    path: '',
    method: '',
    statusCode: '',
    minDurationSeconds: '',
    username: '',
    projectName: ''
  })
  return applyFilters()
}

/* ── Loading ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
let round = null
let loadSeq = 0

/**
 * A single aggregation that has not answered within this window is dropped.
 *
 * The six panels are independent queries: without a timeout ONE wedged request
 * (a heavy `/by-endpoint` over a whole day, a dropped connection) would keep
 * the whole round pending forever — the dashboard would stay frozen on its
 * previous snapshot, with no error and no spinner to explain it.
 */
const REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS = 30 * 1000

/**
 * Two loads requested within this window AND with the same filters are
 * coalesced (the Live tick and a manual Refresh usually land together). A load
 * with a different filter signature — the operator just changed the bucket
 * size — is never coalesced: it replaces the running round.
 */
const DUPLICATE_ROUND_WINDOW_MS = 1000

/** Identity of the current selection, used to coalesce identical loads only. */
function roundKey() {
  return `${state.filters.grouping}|${JSON.stringify(buildFilters())}`
}

/**
 * Races `task` against a clock. The rejection carries a readable message so it
 * can be shown in the banner as-is.
 *
 * @param {Promise<any>|any} task Request to track.
 * @param {string} label Human name of the panel (used in the message).
 * @param {number} [timeoutMs]
 * @returns {Promise<any>}
 */
function withTimeout(task, label, timeoutMs = REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS) {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    const timer = setTimeout(
      () => reject(new Error(`${label} did not answer within ${Math.round(timeoutMs / 1000)}s`)),
      timeoutMs
    )
    Promise.resolve(task).then(
      (value) => {
        clearTimeout(timer)
        resolve(value)
      },
      (error) => {
        clearTimeout(timer)
        reject(error)
      }
    )
  })
}

/**
 * Writes one aggregation into the state. Called per slot, as soon as that slot
 * answers — this is what makes the chart react to a refresh instead of waiting
 * for the slowest table.
 */
function applyResult(slot, value) {
  switch (slot) {
    case 'summary':
      state.summary = value || null
      break
    case 'daily':
      state.series = Array.isArray(value) ? value : []
      break
    case 'endpoints':
      state.endpoints = value || {}
      // Only refresh the option list when no path filter is narrowing it.
      if (!state.filters.path) {
        state.endpointOptions = Object.keys(state.endpoints).sort()
      }
      break
    case 'statuses':
      state.statuses = value || {}
      break
    case 'slowest':
      state.slowest = Array.isArray(value) ? value : []
      break
    case 'errors':
      state.errors = Array.isArray(value) ? value : []
      break
    default:
      break
  }
}

/**
 * Reloads every panel of the view in one round (all requests in parallel).
 *
 * @param {{silent?: boolean}} [options] `silent` skips the loading/error state
 *        flip — used by the auto-refresh loop so the screen never blinks.
 */
export function applyFilters(options) {
  return loadAll(options)
}

export function loadAll({ silent = false } = {}) {
  const now = Date.now()
  const key = roundKey()

  // Burst of identical requests (Live tick + Refresh click): reuse the round
  // that is already fetching the very same selection.
  if (round && round.key === key && now - round.startedAt < DUPLICATE_ROUND_WINDOW_MS) {
    return round.promise
  }

  const filters = buildFilters()
  const grouping = state.filters.grouping
  const client = scopedApi()

  if (!silent) {
    state.error = null
    if (state.status !== 'ready') state.status = 'loading'
  }

  const seq = ++loadSeq

  const tasks = {
    summary: () => client.summary(filters),
    daily: () => client.daily({ ...filters, grouping }),
    endpoints: () => client.byEndpoint(filters),
    statuses: () => client.byStatus(filters),
    slowest: () => client.slowest({ ...filters, limit: SLOWEST_LIMIT }),
    errors: () => client.errors({ ...filters, limit: ERRORS_LIMIT })
  }

  const labels = {
    summary: 'summary',
    daily: 'chart series',
    endpoints: 'endpoints',
    statuses: 'status codes',
    slowest: 'slowest calls',
    errors: 'failed calls'
  }

  const promise = (async () => {
    const failures = []

    await Promise.all(
      Object.entries(tasks).map(([slot, task]) => (
        withTimeout(task(), labels[slot])
          .then((value) => {
            if (seq === loadSeq) applyResult(slot, value)
          })
          .catch((error) => {
            // A newer round owns the state: its results must not be mixed with
            // the answer of an outdated selection.
            failures.push(error?.message || String(error))
          })
      ))
    )

    if (seq !== loadSeq) return

    state.status = 'ready'
    state.error = failures.length ? failures[0] : null
    state.lastUpdated = new Date()
    // Explicit "the numbers changed" signal for the tables and the canvas.
    state.revision += 1
  })()

  const release = () => {
    if (round && round.seq === seq) round = null
  }
  promise.then(release, release)

  round = { seq, key, startedAt: now, promise }
  return promise
}

/** Loads the dates that actually contain data (used as a hint in the header). */
export async function loadDates() {
  try {
    state.dates = await scopedApi().dates()
  } catch (err) {
    state.dates = []
  }
  return state.dates
}

/** Switches between the user-scoped and the admin endpoint set. */
export function setAllUsers(enabled) {
  state.allUsers = !!enabled
  // A different scope means a different date set: refresh both.
  loadDates()
  return loadAll()
}

/* ── Auto-refresh ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
let timer = null

export function stopLive() {
  if (timer) {
    clearInterval(timer)
    timer = null
  }
}

function startLive() {
  stopLive()
  if (!state.live) return
  timer = setInterval(() => loadAll({ silent: true }), intervalMs())
}

function intervalMs() {
  const seconds = Number(state.interval) || DEFAULT_INTERVAL
  return Math.max(5, seconds) * 1000
}

/** Header "Live" switch. */
export function setLive(on) {
  state.live = !!on
  if (state.live) {
    loadAll({ silent: true })
    startLive()
  } else {
    stopLive()
  }
}

/** Header interval selector: reschedules when live, otherwise just remembers. */
export function setIntervalSeconds(seconds) {
  const value = Number(seconds)
  state.interval = REFRESH_INTERVALS.some(o => o.value === value) ? value : DEFAULT_INTERVAL
  if (state.live) {
    loadAll({ silent: true })
    startLive()
  }
}

/* ── Derived data ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */

/** Numeric value used to order the endpoint table. */
function sortValue(row) {
  const values = {
    calls: Number(row.calls) || 0,
    errors: Number(row.errors) || 0,
    avg: Number(row.avg_duration_seconds) || 0,
    p95: Number(row.p95_duration_seconds) || 0,
    max: Number(row.max_duration_seconds) || 0
  }
  return values[state.sortBy] ?? 0
}

/** Endpoints as a sorted array `[{ path, calls, errors, avg_duration_seconds… }]`. */
export const endpointRows = computed(() =>
  Object.entries(state.endpoints || {})
    .map(([path, metrics]) => ({ path, ...metrics }))
    .sort((a, b) => sortValue(b) - sortValue(a))
)

/** Status codes as an array ordered numerically. */
export const statusRows = computed(() =>
  Object.entries(state.statuses || {})
    .map(([code, metrics]) => ({ code: Number(code), ...metrics }))
    .sort((a, b) => a.code - b.code)
)

/** Chart input: period label + counts + latencies in ms. */
export const chartRows = computed(() =>
  (state.series || []).map((entry) => ({
    label: formatPeriod(entry.period),
    calls: Number(entry.calls) || 0,
    errors: Number(entry.errors) || 0,
    avg_ms: Math.round((Number(entry.avg_duration_seconds) || 0) * 1000),
    p95_ms: Math.round((Number(entry.p95_duration_seconds) || 0) * 1000)
  }))
)

/** False until the backend reported at least one call. */
export const hasData = computed(() => (Number(state.summary?.calls) || 0) > 0)

/* ── Client logs: socket events + local network ───────────────────────────── */

/**
 * Raw browser-side logs, newest first, straight from `@/api/telemetry`.
 * They are independent from the backend aggregates: a failed reload does not
 * touch them, and they are cleared explicitly from the cards.
 */
export const socketEvents = computed(() => telemetry.socket)
export const localNetworkCalls = computed(() => telemetry.network)

/**
 * Cumulative socket counters (they survive the ring-buffer rotation, so the
 * totals never shrink while the session lasts) + the connection state.
 */
export const socketTotals = computed(() => {
  const counters = telemetry.socketCounters
  return {
    connected: telemetry.socketConnected,
    socketId: telemetry.socketId,
    total: counters.total,
    sent: counters.sent,
    received: counters.received,
    system: counters.system,
    errors: counters.errors,
    avgAckMs: counters.acked ? counters.ackTotalMs / counters.acked : 0,
    lastAt: telemetry.lastSocketAt
  }
})

/** Cumulative local-network counters, with the average measured latency. */
export const localNetworkTotals = computed(() => {
  const counters = telemetry.networkCounters
  const calls = counters.calls
  return {
    calls,
    errors: counters.errors,
    errorRate: calls ? counters.errors / calls : 0,
    avgMs: calls ? counters.totalMs / calls : 0,
    slow: counters.slow,
    lastAt: telemetry.lastNetworkAt
  }
})

/** Socket events grouped by name, busiest first. */
export const socketEventRows = computed(() => {
  const map = new Map()

  for (const entry of telemetry.socket) {
    const row = map.get(entry.event) || {
      event: entry.event,
      calls: 0,
      sent: 0,
      received: 0,
      errors: 0,
      ackTotalMs: 0,
      acked: 0,
      lastAt: 0
    }
    row.calls += 1
    if (entry.direction === 'out') row.sent += 1
    else if (entry.direction === 'in') row.received += 1
    if (entry.error) row.errors += 1
    if (entry.durationMs !== null) {
      row.ackTotalMs += entry.durationMs
      row.acked += 1
    }
    row.lastAt = Math.max(row.lastAt, entry.ts || 0)
    map.set(entry.event, row)
  }

  return [...map.values()]
    .map(row => ({ ...row, avgAckMs: row.acked ? row.ackTotalMs / row.acked : 0 }))
    .sort((a, b) => b.calls - a.calls)
})

/** Local network calls grouped by method + path, slowest first. */
export const localNetworkRows = computed(() => {
  const map = new Map()

  for (const entry of telemetry.network) {
    const key = `${entry.method} ${entry.path}`
    const row = map.get(key) || {
      key,
      method: entry.method,
      path: entry.path,
      calls: 0,
      errors: 0,
      totalMs: 0,
      maxMs: 0,
      lastStatus: 0,
      lastAt: 0
    }
    row.calls += 1
    if (entry.error || entry.status >= 500) row.errors += 1
    row.totalMs += entry.durationMs || 0
    row.maxMs = Math.max(row.maxMs, entry.durationMs || 0)
    row.lastAt = Math.max(row.lastAt, entry.ts || 0)
    if (entry.ts >= row.lastAt) row.lastStatus = entry.status || 0
    map.set(key, row)
  }

  return [...map.values()]
    .map(row => ({ ...row, avgMs: row.calls ? row.totalMs / row.calls : 0 }))
    .sort((a, b) => b.avgMs - a.avgMs)
})

/** Empties one of the client-side logs (or both). */
export function clearClientLogs(scope = 'all') {
  clearClientTelemetry(scope)
}

/* ── Diagnostics ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
const TONE_ORDER = { error: 0, warning: 1, info: 2 }

/**
 * Deterministic rules over the current aggregate — the "what should I look at
 * first" list. Every entry is derived from a measured value; nothing here is a
 * guess.
 */
export const issues = computed(() => {
  const list = []
  const summary = state.summary || {}
  const calls = Number(summary.calls) || 0

  if (!calls) return list

  const errorRate = Number(summary.error_rate) || 0
  const p95 = Number(summary.p95_duration_seconds) || 0
  const avg = Number(summary.avg_duration_seconds) || 0

  if (errorRate >= 0.05) {
    list.push({
      id: 'errors',
      tone: 'error',
      icon: 'fa-circle-exclamation',
      title: `${fmtPct(errorRate)} of the calls failed`,
      detail: `${Number(summary.errors) || 0} of ${fmtNum(calls)} calls returned an error or a 5xx.`
    })
  } else if (errorRate >= 0.01) {
    list.push({
      id: 'errors',
      tone: 'warning',
      icon: 'fa-triangle-exclamation',
      title: `Error rate at ${fmtPct(errorRate)}`,
      detail: 'Above the 1% threshold — check the failed calls list.'
    })
  }

  if (p95 >= 2) {
    list.push({
      id: 'p95',
      tone: 'error',
      icon: 'fa-gauge-high',
      title: `p95 latency is ${fmtMs(p95)}`,
      detail: '1 in 20 requests waits longer than this — users feel it.'
    })
  } else if (p95 >= 0.8) {
    list.push({
      id: 'p95',
      tone: 'warning',
      icon: 'fa-gauge-high',
      title: `p95 latency is ${fmtMs(p95)}`,
      detail: 'The slow tail is growing. Look at the slowest endpoints.'
    })
  }

  if (avg >= 0.5) {
    list.push({
      id: 'avg',
      tone: 'warning',
      icon: 'fa-clock',
      title: `Average latency is ${fmtMs(avg)}`,
      detail: 'Every request pays this — it multiplies the whole UI.'
    })
  }

  const worst = endpointRows.value[0]
  const worstP95 = Number(worst?.p95_duration_seconds) || 0
  if (worst && worstP95 >= 0.8) {
    list.push({
      id: 'endpoint',
      tone: worstP95 >= 2 ? 'error' : 'warning',
      icon: 'fa-route',
      title: `Slowest endpoint: ${worst.path}`,
      detail: `p95 ${fmtMs(worstP95)} over ${fmtNum(worst.calls)} calls.`
    })
  }

  const serverErrors = statusRows.value.filter(row => row.code >= 500)
  if (serverErrors.length) {
    const total = serverErrors.reduce((sum, row) => sum + (Number(row.calls) || 0), 0)
    list.push({
      id: 'status',
      tone: 'error',
      icon: 'fa-server',
      title: `${fmtNum(total)} server error response(s)`,
      detail: serverErrors.map(row => `${row.code}: ${fmtNum(row.calls)}`).join(' · ')
    })
  }

  // Client-side signals: a dropped socket pauses every live update, and a
  // failing LAN hop is invisible to the server-side aggregates.
  const socketCounters = telemetry.socketCounters
  if (socketCounters.total && !telemetry.socketConnected) {
    list.push({
      id: 'socket-offline',
      tone: 'warning',
      icon: 'fa-plug-circle-xmark',
      title: 'Socket disconnected',
      detail: 'Live updates are paused until this tab reconnects.'
    })
  }
  if (socketCounters.errors) {
    list.push({
      id: 'socket-errors',
      tone: 'error',
      icon: 'fa-bolt',
      title: `${fmtNum(socketCounters.errors)} socket event error(s)`,
      detail: 'An event was emitted while disconnected or the connection failed.'
    })
  }
  if (telemetry.networkCounters.errors) {
    list.push({
      id: 'local-network',
      tone: 'warning',
      icon: 'fa-network-wired',
      title: `${fmtNum(telemetry.networkCounters.errors)} local network call(s) failed`,
      detail: `Measured by this browser over ${fmtNum(telemetry.networkCounters.calls)} call(s).`
    })
  }

  return list.sort((a, b) => TONE_ORDER[a.tone] - TONE_ORDER[b.tone])
})

/* ── Formatting ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */

/**
 * Bucket label → readable axis label.
 *
 * Backend periods look like "YYYY-MM-DD", "YYYY-MM-DD HH:00", "YYYY-MM-DD
 * HH:MM" or "YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS" (not ISO-8601), so they are parsed by hand:
 *   • day          → MM-DD
 *   • hour/minute  → keep the day when the bucket starts a new hour, so a
 *                    multi-day range stays readable
 *   • second level → time only (HH:MM:SS): at 5/10/30 s the date would repeat
 *                    on every tick and the axis would become unreadable
 */
export function formatPeriod(period) {
  const value = String(period || '')
  if (!value) return ''
  const [date, time] = value.split(' ')
  if (!time) return date.slice(5) // MM-DD
  const parts = time.split(':')
  if (parts.length >= 3) return time // HH:MM:SS
  // Hourly buckets end in ":00": keep the day so a multi-day range is readable.
  return time.endsWith(':00') ? `${date.slice(5)} ${time}` : time
}

/** Seconds → latency string ("0 ms", "480 ms", "1.24 s"). */
export function fmtMs(seconds) {
  const value = Number(seconds) || 0
  if (!value) return '0 ms'
  if (value < 1) return `${Math.round(value * 1000)} ms`
  return `${value.toFixed(2)} s`
}

/** Seconds → accumulated duration ("90 s", "5m 30s", "2h 10m"). */
export function fmtDuration(seconds) {
  const value = Number(seconds) || 0
  if (!value) return '0 s'
  if (value < 60) return `${value.toFixed(1)} s`
  const minutes = Math.floor(value / 60)
  if (minutes < 60) return `${minutes}m ${Math.round(value % 60)}s`
  return `${Math.floor(minutes / 60)}h ${minutes % 60}m`
}

/** 0..1 ratio → "%". */
export function fmtPct(ratio) {
  const value = (Number(ratio) || 0) * 100
  if (!value) return '0%'
  return `${value < 1 ? value.toFixed(2) : value.toFixed(1)}%`
}

/** Compact integers ("1.2K", "3.4M"). */
export function fmtNum(value) {
  const num = Number(value) || 0
  if (num >= 1_000_000) return `${(num / 1_000_000).toFixed(1)}M`
  if (num >= 1_000) return `${(num / 1_000).toFixed(1)}K`
  return String(num)
}

/** Unix seconds (stored on the event) → readable timestamp. */
export function fmtTimestamp(unixSeconds) {
  const seconds = Number(unixSeconds) || 0
  if (!seconds) return '—'
  const date = new Date(seconds * 1000)
  if (isNaN(date.getTime())) return '—'
  return `${date.toLocaleDateString()} ${date.toLocaleTimeString()}`
}

/**
 * Epoch MILLISECONDS → "HH:MM:SS".
 * The client-side logs are stamped with `Date.now()`, unlike the backend events
 * which carry unix seconds (see `fmtTimestamp`), and in a live table the time of
 * day is what identifies a tick.
 */
export function fmtClock(ms) {
  const value = Number(ms) || 0
  if (!value) return '—'
  const date = new Date(value)
  if (isNaN(date.getTime())) return '—'
  return date.toLocaleTimeString()
}

/** Tone for an error ratio. */
export function errorTone(ratio) {
  const value = Number(ratio) || 0
  if (value >= 0.05) return 'error'
  if (value >= 0.01) return 'warning'
  return 'success'
}

/** Tone for a latency in seconds. */
export function latencyTone(seconds) {
  const value = Number(seconds) || 0
  if (value >= 2) return 'error'
  if (value >= 0.5) return 'warning'
  return 'success'
}

/** Tone for an HTTP status code. */
export function statusTone(code) {
  const value = Number(code) || 0
  if (value >= 500) return 'error'
  if (value >= 400) return 'warning'
  if (value >= 300) return 'info'
  return 'success'
}

/* ── Composable accessor ──────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
export function useApiMonitoring() {
  return {
    state,
    // configuration
    GROUPINGS,
    DEFAULT_GROUPING,
    REFRESH_INTERVALS,
    DATE_PRESETS,
    METHODS,
    CHART_METRICS,
    SLOWEST_LIMIT,
    ERRORS_LIMIT,
    TOP_ENDPOINTS,
    TOP_SLOWEST,
    TOP_CLIENT_ROWS,
